1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Lena Larsson was a Swedish interior designer and cabinetmaker who redefined post-war domestic life by focusing on functional, family-oriented environments. Born into the Rabenius family in 1919, she trained under C
2 CONC arl Malmsten before conducting extensive sociological research on how housewives actually used their living spaces. This data-driven approach informed her work at Nordiska Kompaniet, where she led the NK-bo and NK-bo NU department
2 CONC s. Larsson is particularly noted for co-designing the TRIVA furniture line in 1944, which introduced affordable, "knock-down" designs that predated the IKEA business model. As the editor of the magazine Allt i hemmet and a frequen
2 CONC t educator, she promoted a "wear-and-tear" philosophy that prioritized practical utility over formal aesthetics. Her collaboration with her husband, architect Mårten Larsson, on projects like the Skal och kärna house further estab
2 CONC lished her as a central figure in modern Swedish design history until her death in 2000.
